==== exit ====
0
==== stdout ====
Usage: raphael status [--json]

Check the connection: which server, from where, and whether it accepts the key.

Uses the connection every remote command uses: RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and
RAPHAEL_API_KEY when both are set, otherwise the saved login.

Asks the server once whether the key is accepted and which protocol and release
it runs. Changes nothing.

Exit 0 means remote commands can work. Otherwise the exit code and error are the
ones the next read would meet: 5 for no connection, a rejected key, a protocol
mismatch, or an address that is not a Raphael server or does not speak HTTP; 6
when the server could not be reached or did not answer in time; 1 when the
server failed. A write that meets the same failure after it was sent exits 7,
because it may have been applied.

Options:
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael status
  raphael status --json
    The check an agent runs first.

Prints:
  The server address, its source, whether the key was accepted, and the protocol
  and release of each side. JSON has connection {endpoint, source, configFile},
  protocolVersion, serverVersion (null when the server does not say) and
  cliVersion.

Next: raphael list /

See also: raphael help connecting
